```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely on router4, not router3 as the team asserted. Router4 is the
default gateway for the Location C subnet (192.168.200.0/24) and sits at the edge of
that segment. Its two uplink interfaces — eth5 (172.25.1.1/24, the link_r4_r3 segment
toward router3) and eth4 (192.168.15.2/24, the link_r4_r5 segment toward router5) —
are both administratively DOWN (no UP or LOWER_UP kernel flags). Because FRR's RIP
process relies on the kernel to install connected routes before it can send or receive
updates on those subnets, both uplinks are entirely invisible to RIP. As a result,
router4's FRR routing table contains exactly one entry: its directly connected
192.168.200.0/24. It has no path to forward any packet destined outside that subnet,
and it responds to every outbound packet from PC11-14 with an ICMP "Destination Net
Unreachable" — confirmed live from 192.168.200.1 during testing.

Router3 is completely healthy: its eth5 (172.25.1.2/24, the router3 side of the same
link) is UP, it carries valid RIP routes to both Location A prefixes (172.16.10.0/24
and 172.16.20.0/24), and it was never the source of the outage. The team's first-hop
bias — seeing that Location C traffic disappears at router3's neighbor — led to a
wrong-device attribution.

Root cause: router4's two uplink interfaces (eth4 and eth5) have been administratively
brought down, completely isolating it from the RIP domain and leaving Location C with
no exit path.

Corrective fix: on router4, run `ip link set eth4 up && ip link set eth5 up`. RIP
adjacencies with router5 and router3 will re-establish within one or two update cycles
(~30-60 seconds), restoring full reachability for all Location C hosts.
=== END ===
```